#!/bin/sh
#
# pre-push: check that what this push sends has already passed the local gate.
#
# It does NOT run the gate. That is the whole point, and the reason is
# mechanical rather than a preference. Git opens its connection to the
# remote BEFORE running this hook, because it needs the remote's ref
# advertisement to build the lines it feeds us on stdin. A hook that then
# runs a twenty minute suite hands git back a connection that died long
# ago, and the push fails with SIGPIPE, exit 141, and no output at all
# while the gate prints "all checks passed". That was reproduced five
# times on this repository before the cause was found.
#
# So the gate is a process a developer runs on its own schedule:
#
#     make push-gate     # or make ci, which is stricter
#     git push
#
# `make push-gate` and `make ci` each write a receipt naming the commit
# they verified. This hook reads it back. A receipt is only issued from a
# clean tree, and only when HEAD did not move while the gate ran, which
# makes this arrangement STRICTER than running the suite here used to be:
# that proved something about the working tree and then pushed commits,
# and with uncommitted edits those are different code.
#
# Everything this hook decides lives in tools/gatereceipt, which reads
# git's own pre-push lines from the stdin passed straight through below:
# one line per ref, `<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>`.
# The parsing is there rather than here because every decision made from
# those four fields is unit tested against real repositories, and shell
# that splits its own input is not. A ref name a loop here mangled would
# fail closed, which reads to a developer as the gate refusing a commit
# that passed.
#
# What it answers, per ref, and what each answer means is in that command's
# own doc comment: the gated commit passes, an annotated tag cut at it
# passes (git names the tag OBJECT here, not the commit), a ref created or
# fast-forwarded to a commit already inside the gated history passes
# because those commits travel with it anyway, a push that only deletes
# refs passes because it sends no code, and everything else is refused
# with the reason and the command that fixes it.
#
# Skip a single push with `git push --no-verify`.

set -eu

exec go run ./tools/gatereceipt verify --push-stdin
